WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.042 --> 00:00:03.169
- [Narrator] Munch's The Scream,

00:00:03.169 --> 00:00:04.629
Van Gogh's Poppy Flowers,

00:00:04.629 --> 00:00:06.840
even the Mona Lisa herself.

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:09.009
They're part of a
collection of masterpieces

00:00:09.009 --> 00:00:10.552
with one thing in common.

00:00:10.552 --> 00:00:11.845
They've all been stolen

00:00:11.845 --> 00:00:14.347
in precise, daring, and occasionally

00:00:14.347 --> 00:00:16.725
even exquisitely planned heists

00:00:16.725 --> 00:00:21.271
involving everything from
contortionists to speedboats.

00:00:21.271 --> 00:00:22.939
We don't approve of what they do,

00:00:22.939 --> 00:00:26.234
but there is a certain
art to being an art thief.

00:00:26.234 --> 00:00:29.529
- You are amazing.
- I know.

00:00:29.529 --> 00:00:30.739
- Wow.

00:00:31.614 --> 00:00:33.825
- [Narrator] Here's a
sketch of seven of the most

00:00:33.825 --> 00:00:35.869
artful art thefts in history

00:00:35.869 --> 00:00:39.831
to get you inspired for the
many heists of Red Notice.

00:00:39.831 --> 00:00:42.584
(dramatic music)

00:00:53.762 --> 00:00:57.140
If there's a lesson from what
happened in Stockholm in 2000,

00:00:57.140 --> 00:01:00.602
it's that sometimes movie
heists aren't that exaggerated.

00:01:00.602 --> 00:01:01.936
Okay, maybe they are a little,

00:01:01.936 --> 00:01:05.356
but like art, art heists can be bold.

00:01:05.356 --> 00:01:08.526
In late December of that
year at around 5:00 pm,

00:01:08.526 --> 00:01:11.988
three gunmen stormed the city's
still-open National Museum.

00:01:11.988 --> 00:01:15.867
As if it were all some
Swedish version of Die Hard,

00:01:15.867 --> 00:01:18.203
one held security and visitors at gunpoint

00:01:18.203 --> 00:01:19.079
on the ground floor,

00:01:19.079 --> 00:01:21.081
while the others raced to grab the art

00:01:21.081 --> 00:01:23.875
picking up two Renoirs and this Rembrandt

00:01:23.875 --> 00:01:26.044
worth at least $46 million.

00:01:26.961 --> 00:01:28.963
But the thieves saved
their greatest flourish

00:01:28.963 --> 00:01:30.048
for their getaway.

00:01:30.048 --> 00:01:32.509
They detonated two car
bombs outside the museum

00:01:32.509 --> 00:01:35.970
blocking access to the
peninsula it sat on,

00:01:35.970 --> 00:01:39.599
all while making their escape
in frickin' speedboats.

00:01:39.599 --> 00:01:42.185
Even their eventual capture was dramatic.

00:01:42.185 --> 00:01:43.603
It took five years,

00:01:43.603 --> 00:01:46.815
but a founding member of
the FBI's Art Crime Team,

00:01:46.815 --> 00:01:48.483
posing as a crooked art dealer,

00:01:48.483 --> 00:01:51.444
finally nabbed the thieves in Denmark.

00:01:51.444 --> 00:01:52.987
- You don't look like a profiler.

00:01:52.987 --> 00:01:54.489
- Yeah, I get that a lot.

00:01:54.489 --> 00:01:56.366
- [Narrator] And you
thought FBI Art Crime Agents

00:01:56.366 --> 00:01:57.867
were just in the movies.

00:01:57.867 --> 00:01:59.577
But also as in art,

00:01:59.577 --> 00:02:03.039
some heists are best when
subtle rather than provocative.

00:02:04.707 --> 00:02:07.794
Witness what went down
literally in Paraguay's capital

00:02:07.794 --> 00:02:10.463
of Asunción in 2002,

00:02:10.463 --> 00:02:12.632
during the most valuable exhibition

00:02:12.632 --> 00:02:16.052
in the history of the country's
National Fine Arts Museum.

00:02:16.052 --> 00:02:18.805
Clearly masterful
thieves using fake names-

00:02:18.805 --> 00:02:19.764
- She's the Bishop.

00:02:19.764 --> 00:02:21.724
- No shit.

00:02:21.724 --> 00:02:23.226
- [Narrator] Rented a nearby storefront

00:02:23.226 --> 00:02:26.312
and from it tunneled 80
feet over and 10 feet down

00:02:26.312 --> 00:02:28.189
to get below the museum.

00:02:28.189 --> 00:02:29.482
After two months of digging,

00:02:29.482 --> 00:02:31.401
they breached the building undetected

00:02:31.401 --> 00:02:34.070
and made off with millions in paintings.

00:02:34.070 --> 00:02:35.780
Nothing has ever been recovered,

00:02:35.780 --> 00:02:39.159
so maybe there's a benefit
to the subtle approach.

00:02:39.993 --> 00:02:41.911
The Dada master, Marcel Duchamp,

00:02:41.911 --> 00:02:44.497
was known for crafting alter egos.

00:02:44.497 --> 00:02:48.918
And disguise is certainly a
brush in the art thief's kit,

00:02:48.918 --> 00:02:51.129
as was seen in Boston in 1990

00:02:51.129 --> 00:02:54.299
in what is still the
largest heist in history.

00:02:54.299 --> 00:02:55.925
On the night of March 18th,

00:02:55.925 --> 00:02:59.387
with rowdy St. Patrick's
Day parties raging outside,

00:02:59.387 --> 00:03:01.639
two young guards buzzed
what appeared to be

00:03:01.639 --> 00:03:04.642
two conspicuously
mustachioed police officers

00:03:04.642 --> 00:03:07.228
into the Isabella Stewart Gardner museum,

00:03:07.228 --> 00:03:11.024
known in the Boston
underworld as a juicy target

00:03:11.024 --> 00:03:12.650
with lax security.

00:03:12.650 --> 00:03:14.944
Once inside, the men tied up the guards

00:03:14.944 --> 00:03:19.199
and then plundered the
museum of some $500 million

00:03:19.199 --> 00:03:21.868
worth of art, 500 million.

00:03:21.868 --> 00:03:24.162
Despite intense police and media scrutiny,

00:03:24.162 --> 00:03:26.789
the frames remain empty.

00:03:26.789 --> 00:03:28.249
The thieves have never been caught

00:03:28.249 --> 00:03:31.628
making those some damn good disguises.

00:03:32.795 --> 00:03:35.798
But just like great art can
come from overlooked people,

00:03:35.798 --> 00:03:40.011
like the much-mocked "Customs
Officer" Henri Rousseau,

00:03:40.011 --> 00:03:43.056
sometimes being ordinary
is the best disguise.

00:03:43.056 --> 00:03:46.517
That seemed to be the case
in Paris in August of 1911,

00:03:46.517 --> 00:03:49.103
when a man emerged from
a closet in the Louvre

00:03:49.103 --> 00:03:50.313
where he'd been hiding that night,

00:03:50.313 --> 00:03:52.732
and strolled out into the Paris morning

00:03:52.732 --> 00:03:55.318
with the Mona Lisa under a white smock,

00:03:55.318 --> 00:03:58.529
the customary uniform
for maintenance workers.

00:03:58.529 --> 00:04:00.073
It's hard to imagine now,

00:04:00.073 --> 00:04:01.658
but the world's most famous painting

00:04:01.658 --> 00:04:03.743
was actually pretty ordinary then,

00:04:03.743 --> 00:04:07.622
so no one noticed it was
missing for 26 whole hours.

00:04:07.622 --> 00:04:09.999
But then it turned into
a national manhunt,

00:04:09.999 --> 00:04:13.795
a matter of French pride that
seemed to implicate everybody.

00:04:13.795 --> 00:04:16.547
Conspiracies ran wild
that American tycoons

00:04:16.547 --> 00:04:18.049
had financed the operation

00:04:18.049 --> 00:04:19.884
to build their personal collections,

00:04:19.884 --> 00:04:23.930
and suspicion even briefly
fell on poor Pablo Picasso,

00:04:23.930 --> 00:04:27.100
who had a few minor stolen
works in his possession.

00:04:27.100 --> 00:04:29.143
But the police interviewed and dismissed

00:04:29.143 --> 00:04:32.480
lowly Vincenzo Peruggia,
an Italian handyman

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:34.357
who had installed glass in the Louvre,

00:04:34.357 --> 00:04:37.610
and who in fact had the Mona Lisa stashed

00:04:37.610 --> 00:04:40.780
in his boardinghouse in a
trunk with a false bottom.

00:04:40.780 --> 00:04:41.864
After two years,

00:04:41.864 --> 00:04:44.117
Peruggia got caught trying
to sell the painting

00:04:44.117 --> 00:04:45.535
to an Italian art dealer,

00:04:45.535 --> 00:04:48.746
although he claimed he was
just trying to repatriate it

00:04:48.746 --> 00:04:49.789
to Italy.

00:04:49.789 --> 00:04:52.083
The thief served just six months in prison

00:04:52.083 --> 00:04:53.584
and was largely forgotten,

00:04:53.584 --> 00:04:57.630
but the Mona Lisa went on
to achieve a timeless fame.

00:04:57.630 --> 00:05:00.216
Even after executing his artful heist,

00:05:00.216 --> 00:05:02.593
Peruggia's problem was figuring out

00:05:02.593 --> 00:05:05.096
what to do with his
masterpiece going forward.

00:05:05.096 --> 00:05:06.514
- You know what, Booth?

00:05:06.514 --> 00:05:07.640
Maybe I don't know you.

00:05:07.640 --> 00:05:09.392
But I've been building a profile on you

00:05:09.392 --> 00:05:11.769
ever since you stole William
Strang's Lady With A Red Hat

00:05:11.769 --> 00:05:13.688
from the Tate back in 2014.

00:05:13.688 --> 00:05:15.189
- You can't prove that was me.

00:05:20.737 --> 00:05:22.447
I got that on Etsy.

00:05:22.447 --> 00:05:24.532
- [Narrator] And that
pops up again and again

00:05:24.532 --> 00:05:25.950
in tales of art heist.

00:05:25.950 --> 00:05:29.287
But especially in Athens, Greece in 2012,

00:05:29.287 --> 00:05:31.497
where a 49 year-old construction worker

00:05:31.497 --> 00:05:32.999
and self-proclaimed art freak

00:05:32.999 --> 00:05:36.419
studied the guards and cameras
in the National Gallery

00:05:36.419 --> 00:05:38.004
for a whole six months.

00:05:38.004 --> 00:05:40.214
Then, one night in January,

00:05:40.214 --> 00:05:42.925
he climbed onto a balcony
and repeatedly tripped

00:05:42.925 --> 00:05:45.553
a small alarm on an unlocked door there,

00:05:45.553 --> 00:05:49.182
causing the annoyed guard
to disable the others.

00:05:49.182 --> 00:05:51.601
The thief took off with
a Picasso and a Mondrian,

00:05:51.601 --> 00:05:52.935
but what to do with them?

00:05:52.935 --> 00:05:56.647
Stolen, known works of art
are almost impossible to sell,

00:05:56.647 --> 00:05:57.982
they're radioactive.

00:05:57.982 --> 00:05:59.901
So, in response to police sniffing around,

00:05:59.901 --> 00:06:02.695
the thief just continued
moving his paintings.

00:06:02.695 --> 00:06:03.988
First to a warehouse,

00:06:03.988 --> 00:06:06.699
then finally to this ravine under a bush

00:06:06.699 --> 00:06:09.077
40 kilometers south of Athens.

00:06:09.077 --> 00:06:10.244
That's where police found them

00:06:10.244 --> 00:06:12.413
when the thief confessed
under questioning.

00:06:12.413 --> 00:06:14.916
So it might not be so weird after all

00:06:14.916 --> 00:06:17.543
to find a bunch of stolen
artwork in the jungle.

00:06:19.587 --> 00:06:21.464
But, for some thieves,
it's not just the heist

00:06:21.464 --> 00:06:22.965
that's elevated to an art form.

00:06:22.965 --> 00:06:24.801
It's the taunting.

00:06:24.801 --> 00:06:27.887
- It's so nice to finally
meet you, Mr. Booth.

00:06:27.887 --> 00:06:31.474
Such a thrill to be face-to-face
with the second-best

00:06:31.474 --> 00:06:33.726
art thief in the world.

00:06:33.726 --> 00:06:35.478
- [Narrator] That was certainly
true of the ringleader

00:06:35.478 --> 00:06:39.732
of the team who hit Oslo's
National Gallery in 1994.

00:06:39.732 --> 00:06:42.151
On the opening day of
the Lillehammer Olympics,

00:06:42.151 --> 00:06:43.361
two men climbed a ladder,

00:06:43.361 --> 00:06:44.987
broke a window, cut some wires,

00:06:44.987 --> 00:06:49.951
and took Edvard Munch's The
Scream all in 50 seconds.

00:06:50.118 --> 00:06:52.495
But they couldn't help but leave a note,

00:06:52.495 --> 00:06:55.415
"A thousand thanks for the bad security."

00:06:55.415 --> 00:06:58.251
That's because it was all the
master plan of Pal Enger,

00:06:58.251 --> 00:07:00.420
a brash former footballer with a passion

00:07:00.420 --> 00:07:01.754
for stealing Munch paintings.

00:07:01.754 --> 00:07:04.924
He'd already stolen Vampire in 1988.

00:07:04.924 --> 00:07:06.968
Enger just couldn't help himself.

00:07:06.968 --> 00:07:08.344
He aroused police suspicion

00:07:08.344 --> 00:07:09.971
when he took out an ad
in the paper announcing

00:07:09.971 --> 00:07:13.516
that his son had been
born "with a scream".

00:07:14.517 --> 00:07:16.477
With the help of
international law enforcement,

00:07:16.477 --> 00:07:19.188
Enger was eventually sentenced
to six years in prison-

00:07:19.188 --> 00:07:20.523
- It's nice to meet a fan.

00:07:20.523 --> 00:07:21.399
- [Narrator] But it's unclear

00:07:21.399 --> 00:07:23.734
whether he's any less outspoken.

00:07:23.734 --> 00:07:27.029
A Facebook page belonging to @PalEngerArt

00:07:27.029 --> 00:07:30.241
still openly posts photos of the heist.

00:07:30.241 --> 00:07:32.160
But other artists of crime

00:07:32.160 --> 00:07:33.661
gained the respect of their audience

00:07:33.661 --> 00:07:36.080
via their quiet craftsmanship.

00:07:36.080 --> 00:07:38.624
It happened in Jerusalem in 1983

00:07:38.624 --> 00:07:41.294
in what would go down as
the costliest art heist

00:07:41.294 --> 00:07:42.837
in Israel's history.

00:07:42.837 --> 00:07:46.591
Housed at the L.A. Mayer
Memorial Museum of Islamic Art,

00:07:46.591 --> 00:07:49.469
the targets were a collection
of priceless timepieces

00:07:49.469 --> 00:07:51.471
that included Marie Antoinette's watch,

00:07:51.471 --> 00:07:54.182
the so-called Mona Lisa
of the clock world.

00:07:54.182 --> 00:07:56.601
One night in April, a thief named Diller

00:07:56.601 --> 00:08:00.062
used a hydraulic jack to bend
the bars on the museum's fence

00:08:00.062 --> 00:08:03.691
and then slithered in through
a tiny window 10 feet up.

00:08:03.691 --> 00:08:06.694
A strategically parked
truck blocked the view.

00:08:06.694 --> 00:08:09.197
He brought an air mattress
to cushion his fall,

00:08:09.197 --> 00:08:11.699
listening devices to monitor the guards,

00:08:11.699 --> 00:08:13.451
even a bag of sandwiches.

00:08:13.451 --> 00:08:15.661
- Sorry, big man, but
there's no food or drink

00:08:15.661 --> 00:08:16.579
in the museum.

00:08:19.290 --> 00:08:20.333
Thank you.

00:08:20.333 --> 00:08:22.126
- [Narrator] So many clocks were taken

00:08:22.126 --> 00:08:24.754
that the police thought
it was a multi-man job.

00:08:24.754 --> 00:08:28.174
And for 25 years the case went unsolved,

00:08:28.174 --> 00:08:30.301
until the thief's widow tried to sell back

00:08:30.301 --> 00:08:32.053
some of his collection.

00:08:32.053 --> 00:08:34.597
What were the investigators
reactions to the news

00:08:34.597 --> 00:08:37.725
that the man they sought so long was dead?

00:08:37.725 --> 00:08:39.685
They called the thief legendary.

00:08:39.685 --> 00:08:41.020
They were actually disappointed

00:08:41.020 --> 00:08:42.605
they would never get to meet him.

00:08:42.605 --> 00:08:45.441
So who says an art thief can't inspire?

00:08:45.441 --> 00:08:47.527
You might have heard
Picasso's famous quote,

00:08:47.527 --> 00:08:49.570
"Art is theft."

00:08:49.570 --> 00:08:52.990
We're not gonna go so far
as saying that theft is art,

00:08:52.990 --> 00:08:56.202
but some have made it look artful,

00:08:56.202 --> 00:08:58.913
both in real life and
the streaming screen.

00:09:00.039 --> 00:09:01.582
See a couple of master thieves

00:09:01.582 --> 00:09:03.417
demonstrate the art of the heist,

00:09:03.417 --> 00:09:04.877
with the police in hot pursuit,

00:09:04.877 --> 00:09:07.421
in Red Notice coming November 12th.

00:09:15.596 --> 00:09:18.182
Of course, not all
thieves can be so artful.

00:09:18.182 --> 00:09:20.476
In Oslo in 2004 at the Munch Museum,

00:09:20.476 --> 00:09:23.020
crowds watched as two clumsy culprits

00:09:23.020 --> 00:09:25.898
ran straight into the
building's plate glass doors.

00:09:25.898 --> 00:09:28.359
But hey, they still managed
to dust themselves off

00:09:28.359 --> 00:09:30.528
and nab a different version of The Scream,

00:09:30.528 --> 00:09:33.239
so who needs grace, anyway?

00:09:33.239 --> 00:09:35.491
(shouting)

